{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Houston Astros arrived with a point to prove, carrying the weight of a franchise forever changed by the sign-stealing scandal. They played like a team with something to settle, finishing with 95 wins and a potent offense that slugged 221 home runs while their pitching staff kept opponents off the board with a 3.78 ERA. What unfolded across 162 games was a season of crescendos — bursts of brilliance that carried a flawed but formidable club toward October with increasing conviction.\n\nApril and May were a study in survival. The Astros mixed clutch moments — Carlos Correa's early homer, a Robel García walk-off single, Jose Altuve's three-run blast — with frustrating bullpen implosions. Bryan Abreu surrendered a devastating three-run homer to Adolis García in Texas, and the San Diego series in late May saw the bullpen cough up leads in excruciating fashion. Yet the team finished both months above .500, grinding out wins even when the high-leverage moments broke against them.\n\nJune and July transformed the Astros from a good team into a great one. They went 19-9 in June and 16-8 in July, their best back-to-back months of the year. Correa delivered a solo shot that felt like a turning point, Altuve hit his 20th home run in a massive three-run blast, and the pitching held firm game after game. Even when Brooks Raley gave up a Dylan Moore grand slam in late July, the offense and rotation absorbed the blow. This was the stretch that announced Houston's championship intentions to the rest of the American League.\n\nAugust and September brought some turbulence — grand slams surrendered, late-game leads squandered — but the Astros never truly wavered, finishing both months with winning records and locking up the AL West. When October arrived, they stormed through the White Sox in the Division Series and outlasted the Red Sox in a dramatic Championship Series that saw Altuve and Jason Castro deliver in crucial moments. The World Series against Atlanta, however, belonged to the Braves. Jorge Soler's three-run homer in the decisive Game 6, bookended by Adam Duvall's grand slam and Dansby Swanson's clutch contributions, denied Houston what would have been a redemption title. The Astros fell one series short — talented, dangerous, and destined to return.",
